Welcome to unkernel
===================

This is a Linux 7.3 kernel compiled to WebAssembly, running in a web
page or in Node.js on your machine; nothing runs on a server. The kernel
is native wasm, and the programs are unmodified aarch64 Linux binaries,
run by kwemu, an aarch64 CPU that is linked into the kernel.

User space is @NAME@ (@USERLAND@).
You are "user", and this is your home directory. sudo works without a
password: "sudo -i" gives a root shell.

Things to try
-------------

  @PKG@ htop
                    Install packages from @MIRROR@ mirrors. Typed
                    in a shell it runs through sudo for you.
  htop              Or just type the command: if it is missing, the
                    package that provides it is installed for you
                    (@AUTO@=0 turns that off).
  claude, opencode, fx, node, bun, uv
                    Installed into ~/.local/bin the first time you run
                    one (claude and opencode are 250-360 MB, so they
                    need the data disk or plenty of memory).
@if debian
  docker run --rm -it alpine
                    Docker is installed and started the first time you
                    run docker. Containers use the guest's network.
@end
  glimmer           A tiny game, with graphics and sound.
@if debian
  kmscube           A spinning cube drawn by your GPU: on the web page
                    the guest has /dev/dri, and Mesa renders through
                    the browser's WebGL2. Any key stops it. X with
                    glamor: Xorg :0 -config kw-kms.conf
@end
  python3 -m http.server 8000
                    A guest web server (on the web page it opens in the
                    preview, Ctrl-A p).
@if alpine

x86_64, riscv64, i386 and arm binaries also run, through qemu-user
(installed the first time one starts). "kw-arch x86_64 CMD" makes
uname report x86_64, for installers that pick their download by it.
@end
@if debian

x86_64 and i386 binaries also run, through box64 (installed the first
time one starts, which takes about a minute).
@end

Your changes
------------

The root file system is an overlay over the read-only @NAME@ image.
With a data disk (the web page keeps one in your browser; the CLI takes
--data FILE) your changes are kept on it and survive a reload or a
reboot; without one they live in memory until power-off. If a change
stops the system from booting, that boot runs in safe mode instead:
your changes stay on the data disk in /data/distros/@DISTRO@/upper,
where you can fix them, and every boot tries them again ("kw-overlay
status" says more, "sudo kw-overlay discard" sets them aside and starts
over from the image).

On the web page
---------------

  Ctrl-A ?          All the keyboard shortcuts (on a Mac, Cmd-A works
                    as well as Ctrl-A).
  Ctrl-A n          Another terminal, up to 9: a tab of the Terminal
                    window (Ctrl-A N opens one in a new window).
                    Ctrl-A 1-9 switches between them.
  Ctrl-Shift-O      The app launcher (Cmd-K on a Mac, or the first
                    tile of the Dock).
@if debian
  foot              Wayland apps started in a terminal show as windows
                    on the page. Ctrl-A w switches between them and the
                    other windows.
@end

Settings (Ctrl-A ,) has the machine's options and export, import or
reset of the disk kept in your browser (IndexedDB); System (Ctrl-A s)
has the kernel log and diagnostics. Open the page with ?data=0 for a
fresh system every time.

Limits
------

- Programs run on an emulated CPU: system calls are fast, but heavy
  computation is several times slower than native.
- No IPv6, and no inbound connections from the internet. On the web
  page the network reaches out through a Wisp proxy.
- No strace or gdb (no ptrace register sets), no seccomp.

Source, docs and the JavaScript SDK: @REPO@
